## Solution

This is exactly what the **Composite Graph API** is for: one `POST /services/data/vXX.0/composite` call whose body contains one *graph* per order.

- **Each graph is a unit of work** – every record in a graph commits or rolls back together, so an order is saved completely or not at all. `graphId` carries the order number, and the graphs are listed in the order given.
- **Graphs are processed independently** – if `SO-1001` fails, `SO-1002` is still committed.
- **The given `referenceId`s** are echoed back per graph in `graphResponse.references`, so the middleware can read the results by them.
- **The Opportunity is nested under the Account record** (`Opportunities` child relationship), so it is created in the same transaction and linked to the account identified by `acct_1001` / `acct_1002`.

One caveat worth stating: Composite Graph supports **create only** — it has no upsert. Since the binding requirement is all‑or‑nothing per order, the Account is created with `Customer_Number__c` populated (a duplicate external ID would fail the graph cleanly rather than create a silent duplicate). If true upsert‑on‑existing-customer were required, you'd have to fall back to the plain Composite resource, which cannot provide per-order atomicity — hence the graph API is the right primitive here (assumption: these are new customers).

```http
POST /services/data/v60.0/composite HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1001",
      "records": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1001",
          "attributes": { "type": "Account" },
          "Name": "Alder Creek Cafe",
          "Customer_Number__c": "C-1001",
          "Opportunities": {
            "records": [
              {
                "referenceId": "opp_1001",
                "attributes": { "type": "Opportunity" },
                "Name": "Web order SO-1001",
                "StageName": "Closed Won",
                "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
                "Amount": 1250
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1002",
      "records": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1002",
          "attributes": { "type": "Account" },
          "Name": "Birch Lane Bakery",
          "Customer_Number__c": "C-2002",
          "Opportunities": {
            "records": [
              {
                "referenceId": "opp_1002",
                "attributes": { "type": "Opportunity" },
                "Name": "Web order SO-1002",
                "StageName": "Closed Won",
                "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
                "Amount": 980
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Approach

The requirement is **all‑or‑nothing**, and a plain Composite API call does **not** roll back subrequests that already succeeded if a later one fails. The right tool is the **SObject Graph (Composite Graph) REST API** (GA since API v53.0): everything in the request runs in a single transaction, records are inserted in dependency order, and **if any record fails, the entire request is rolled back**.

Key points of the payload:

- **Graph 1** roots on the Account and nests the Asset under the `Assets` child relationship, so the Account is inserted first and the Asset's `AccountId` is populated automatically. `referenceId` values go in each record's `attributes`.
- **Graph 2** roots on `Service_Request__c`, which is unrelated to the other two objects — unrelated objects go in separate graphs in the same request.
- Graphs are processed in the order listed, all in one transaction. The Service Request insert happens after the Asset insert, and the trigger's SOQL on `Asset` will see the uncommitted (same‑transaction) record with `SerialNumber = SN-88213`, so validation passes.
- The response returns each saved record by its `referenceId` (`newAccount`, `newAsset`, `newRequest`), which is what the middleware reads. If any record fails, nothing is saved and an error is returned.

```http
POST /services/data/v62.0/composite/graph HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "onboarding",
      "record": {
        "attributes": {
          "type": "Account",
          "referenceId": "newAccount"
        },
        "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply",
        "Assets": {
          "attributes": {
            "type": "Asset",
            "referenceId": "newAsset"
          },
          "Name": "Trailhead Kiosk 3000",
          "SerialNumber": "SN-88213"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "graphId": "serviceRequest",
      "record": {
        "attributes": {
          "type": "Service_Request__c",
          "referenceId": "newRequest"
        },
        "Subject__c": "Installation",
        "Serial_Number__c": "SN-88213"
      }
    }
  ]
}
