 * Creates follow-up Tasks for opportunities coming out of the weekly pipeline review.
 *
 * Semantics required by the business:
 *  - One Task per opportunity, owned by the opportunity's Owner.
 *  - A task that fails (e.g. rejected by a Task validation rule) must not block the others.
 *  - Returns the Ids of the opportunities whose task could NOT be created
 *    (empty set when everything succeeded).
 *
 * Implementation note: rather than issuing one DML statement per opportunity inside
 * try/catch, we build all tasks and insert them with Database.insert(tasks, false).
 * With allOrNone=false, Salesforce evaluates every row independently: rows that fail
 * validation (or any other row-level error) are rejected individually while all other
 * rows are committed. This gives exactly the per-row error isolation the requirement
 * asks for, in a single DML statement. See the explanation below for why the literal
 * "insert in a loop" version is not safe at the stated volume of 150-250 opportunities.
 */
public inherited sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    private static final Integer DUE_IN_DAYS = 7;
    private static final String STATUS_NOT_STARTED = 'Not Started';
    private static final String PRIORITY_NORMAL = 'Normal';

    /**
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-up tasks for.
     * @return Ids of the opportunities whose task could not be created. This includes
     *         Ids that were null/duplicated in the input, Ids that do not correspond
     *         to an existing (queryable) Opportunity, and Ids whose task insert failed.
     *         Returns an empty set when every task was created successfully.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failed = new Set<Id>();

        // Guard: nothing to do.
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failed;
        }

        // Dedupe and drop nulls so one caller mistake neither creates duplicate
        // tasks nor fails the whole run.
        Set<Id> requested = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            if (oppId != null) {
                requested.add(oppId);
            }
        }
        if (requested.isEmpty()) {
            return failed;
        }

        // Query what we need for the subjects and owners in one SOQL call.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunities = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :requested
        ]);

        // Anything requested but not queryable (deleted, wrong object type, etc.)
        // cannot get a task, so it belongs in the failure set.
        Set<Id> missing = new Set<Id>(requested);
        missing.removeAll(opportunities.keySet());
        failed.addAll(missing);

        if (opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return failed;
        }

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        // Database.insert returns SaveResults in the same order as the input list,
        // so we keep a parallel list to map each result back to its opportunity.
        List<Id> oppIdByIndex = new List<Id>();

        for (Id oppId : opportunities.keySet()) {
            Opportunity opp = opportunities.get(oppId);
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = oppId,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(DUE_IN_DAYS),
                Status = STATUS_NOT_STARTED,
                Priority = PRIORITY_NORMAL
            ));
            oppIdByIndex.add(oppId);
        }

        // allOrNone = false: per-row partial success. A row rejected by a validation
        // rule does not prevent the other rows from being committed, and no
        // DmlException is thrown — the failure is reported via the SaveResult.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failed.add(oppIdByIndex[i]);
                // results[i].getErrors() is available here if compliance ever needs
                // the rejection reason logged; for now the contract only asks for Ids.
            }
        }

        return failed;
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

You asked for the "straightforward" version — loop over the opportunities and `insert` each task on its own inside a try/catch. I did not implement it literally, because at the volume you described it **will throw a runtime error**:

**1. The DML statement limit (150) makes the literal loop a defect.** A trigger-style `insert` per row inside a loop consumes one DML statement per iteration. Your reviews cover **150–250 opportunities**, so on any review of 151+ the method would die with `System.LimitException: Too many DML statements: 151` — and since a `LimitException` cannot be caught, it would roll back everything the caller had done in the transaction. A method that fails on the upper half of its expected input range isn't shippable.

**2. I got the same per-row error isolation with one DML statement.** `Database.insert(tasks, false)` (partial success) evaluates every row independently: a task rejected by a compliance validation rule is rejected alone, all other tasks still commit, and no exception is thrown. I keep a parallel `List<Id>` because the returned `SaveResult` list is order-matched to the input list, which lets me map each failure back to the originating opportunity. That is exactly the contract you specified — failed opportunity Ids returned, successes unaffected — just in one statement instead of up to 250.

**3. Small robustness additions that cost nothing:**
- The input is deduped and nulls are dropped, so a sloppy caller can't create duplicate tasks or poison the run.
- Ids that don't resolve to a queryable Opportunity (deleted rows, wrong object type) are treated as failures and returned, since their task genuinely could not be created.
- The `Opportunity` names and owners are fetched with a single `SOQL IN` query instead of one query per record, so the method stays well inside query limits too (one SOQL, one DML for 250 records).

If you truly need the literal per-row `try/catch` version (e.g., a standard requires it verbatim), it works correctly up to 150 opportunities — but 151+ is a hard, uncatchable failure, which is why I'd put this version forward for the production org.

Here's the implementation, plus one important deviation I made and why.

**Deviation: one `@future` call per *transaction*, not per opportunity.**

You asked for `recalculate(opp.AccountId)` per opportunity. The problem: Salesforce limits each transaction to **50 `@future` calls** (you'd get a `LimitException: Too many future calls`). Your CPQ integration inserts/updates opportunities in batches of 200 — that's 200 future calls, so the trigger would blow up mid-batch every time. I instead collect the distinct `AccountId`s across the trigger and issue a **single `@future` call with a `Set<Id>`**. Same async behavior, saves stay fast, and it's actually less work for the platform (one async transaction, one SOQL pass, one DML). If you truly need the per-opportunity signature, a convenience overload is included. I also recalculate the old account when `AccountId` changes so the previous owner's rating doesn't go stale.

