public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead, returns the Id of the first contact (in contacts order)
     * whose email matches the lead's email, or null if there is no match.
     *
     * Matching rules:
     *  - Case-insensitive, leading/trailing whitespace is ignored.
     *  - Blank (null, empty, or whitespace-only) emails never match.
     *  - On multiple contacts with the same email, the first in list order wins.
     *
     * No SOQL, no DML: operates purely on the lists passed in, so it is safe
     * to call inside the import transaction. O(leads x contacts), which is
     * at most ~9,000,000 comparisons at the stated limits (3,000 x 3,000);
     * a string comparison on a short email is cheap, so this stays well
     * within a single transaction's time budget. If the volumes ever grow
     * well beyond that, swap the inner loop for a Map<String, Id> keyed by
     * the normalized email to make it O(leads + contacts).
     *
     * @param leads    leads to match; not modified.
     * @param contacts contacts to match against; not modified.
     * @return list parallel to leads, same size and order, contact Id or null.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        // Pre-normalize contact emails once (O(contacts)) instead of
        // re-trimming/lowercasing them for every lead.
        List<String> normalizedContactEmails = new List<String>(contacts.size());
        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            normalizedContactEmails.add(normalizeEmail(contact.Email));
        }

        List<Id> contactIds = new List<Id>(leads.size());
        for (Lead lead : leads) {
            String leadEmail = normalizeEmail(lead.Email);
            Id matchedContactId = null;

            if (String.isNotBlank(leadEmail)) {
                for (Integer i = 0; i < contacts.size(); i++) {
                    String contactEmail = normalizedContactEmails[i];
                    if (String.isNotBlank(contactEmail) && contactEmail.equals(leadEmail)) {
                        matchedContactId = contacts[i].Id;
                        break; // first contact in list order wins
                    }
                }
            }

            contactIds.add(matchedContactId);
        }

        return contactIds;
    }

    /**
     * Lowercases and trims an email for comparison purposes only.
     * Returns null for blank input, which never matches anything.
     */
    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

On doing it differently: I kept the nested loop as requested, but with two cheap optimizations that keep it readable — contact emails are normalized once up front rather than re-trimmed per lead, and the inner loop `break`s on first hit. If the lists ever grow beyond the stated limits (e.g., a national import at 50k × 50k), I'd replace the inner loop with a `Map<String, Id>` built from `contacts` (first-write-wins preserves the "first in contacts order" rule), turning the whole thing O(n + m) with no query or DML — same contract, drop-in change. Also note this deliberately does no dedup or fuzzy matching (name-based, `__c` fallback fields); if Marketing later wants "match even when one side has a typo," that's a different, riskier feature that should be explicitly scoped.

